            <ArticleTitle>Association of baseline high-density lipoprotein levels with restenosis after coronary stenting: a meta-analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>386-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MCA.0b013e328362ba8a</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Previous studies have reported inconsistent results on the association between serum baseline high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) levels and in-stent restenosis (ISR) after coronary stenting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We searched for studies without language restriction in PubMed, Embase, and the Cochrane library before June 2012. Inverse random weighted meta-analysis was carried out using logarithm of the odds ratio of ISR with its SE to assess the strength of association in each study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Eight observational studies involving 422 ISR and 1113 non-ISR patients were eligible for our analysis. Overall, there was no association between baseline HDL-C levels and ISR (odds ratio: 1.00, 95% confidence interval: 0.96-1.04). The heterogeneity was not significant between individual studies (P=0.074, I=44.0%) and no publication bias was detected. A sensitivity analysis further confirmed the robustness of results. A similar association was detected in the subgroups with bare metal stent and drug-eluting stent implantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our meta-analysis suggests that serum baseline HDL-C levels are not associated with ISR.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
